# What this tree is linted with.
#
# The set is curated rather than `*`: a wall of findings gets half-disabled on
# the first day and tells nobody anything afterwards. These eight families are
# what this library can hold clean, and tightening from here is a decision
# somebody makes on purpose.
#
# **Every disable below says why.** A disable with no reason is indistinguishable
# from an oversight, and the next person cannot tell whether it was considered.
#
# Several of them are wrong *for this codebase* rather than wrong in general.
# This library's whole job is to sit between an embedder and two C engines, so a
# raw pointer into an engine, a cast at that boundary and a namespace-scope
# engine descriptor struct are the domain, not a defect. Where a finding was a
# real one it was fixed; where it was deliberate it carries a NOLINT naming the
# check and the reason, which is narrower than anything here.
#
# Running it: a Visual Studio build tree has no compile database, so configure
# the `tidy-v8` / `tidy-spidermonkey` presets - Ninja, the same ClangCL
# toolchain - and drive LLVM's own `run-clang-tidy` from one. docs/testing.md
# has the three commands.
Checks: >
  -*,
  bugprone-*,
  cert-*,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  cppcoreguidelines-*,
  modernize-*,
  performance-*,
  portability-*,
  readability-*,

  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-exception-escape,
  -cert-err33-c,
  -cert-err58-cpp,
  -clang-analyzer-optin.core.EnumCastOutOfRange,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-c-arrays,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-magic-numbers,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-non-const-global-variables,
  -cppcoreguidelines-no-malloc,
  -cppcoreguidelines-owning-memory,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-array-to-pointer-decay,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-constant-array-index,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-pointer-arithmetic,
  -cppcoreguidelines-macro-usage,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-const-cast,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-member-init,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-reinterpret-cast,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-vararg,
  -modernize-avoid-c-arrays,
  -modernize-use-designated-initializers,
  -modernize-use-nodiscard,
  -modernize-use-std-print,
  -modernize-use-trailing-return-type,
  -readability-function-cognitive-complexity,
  -readability-identifier-length,
  -readability-magic-numbers,
  -readability-use-anyofallof

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why each of those is off
#
# bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters
#   Reports any two adjacent parameters of the same type. The shapes it fires on
#   - `(const Context&, Slot, Slot)`, `(Isolate&, SlotIndex, SlotIndex)` - are
#   what the engines' own calls take, and the answer it wants is a wrapper
#   struct per pair across a hundred entry points.
#
# bugprone-exception-escape
#   Two shapes, seven findings, and in both the `noexcept` is the decision
#   rather than an oversight. `Isolate::RequestInterrupt` and `Isolate::PostJob`
#   are declared `noexcept` in `unibind/isolate.h`: they are callable from any
#   thread and return nothing, so there is no way to report an allocation
#   failure and terminating is what the header promises. `BackendBuildId` is the
#   same, over one short string built once. The other two are `main`, where an
#   escaping exception is a broken run and the runtime saying so unhandled beats
#   a `catch` that swallows it.
#
# cert-err33-c
#   Its checked-function list is the C standard library catalogue, printf family
#   included. The only places it fires are a benchmark's diagnostics on stderr,
#   on a path that is already returning failure. `bugprone-unused-return-value`
#   stays on for the cases that matter, with `AllowCastToVoid` below.
#
# cert-err58-cpp
#   Fires on the engines' namespace-scope descriptor structs - `JSClass`, the
#   proxy handler - which SpiderMonkey requires to outlive every isolate, and
#   which are constant-initialized from literals and pointers. There is nowhere
#   else to put them and nothing in them that throws.
#
# clang-analyzer-optin.core.EnumCastOutOfRange
#   Assumes an enum's valid values are its enumerators. `PropertyAttribute` and
#   V8's `PropertyFilter` are bitmask enums, where an OR of two enumerators is
#   the normal value and is exactly what the cast is for.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-avoid-c-arrays, modernize-avoid-c-arrays
#   Every finding is *raw storage* rather than an array of objects: the bytes a
#   `HandleScope`, a `TryCatch` and a `ContextScope` reserve on the caller's
#   stack for the backend to placement-new its frame into, plus the frame's own
#   fixed run of slots. `std::array` is layout-identical and would change
#   nothing but the spelling - at the price of `<array>` in `detail/slot.h`,
#   which today includes `<cstdint>` and nothing else, and a `.data()` at every
#   use of a buffer whose whole point is that it is untyped bytes.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-avoid-magic-numbers, readability-magic-numbers
#   The literals it finds are a UTF-8 decoder's masks (0xC0, 0x80, 0x3F, and the
#   shifts beside them) and a test's expected answer. Both are clearer as the
#   number than as a name standing in front of it.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-avoid-non-const-global-variables
#   Both engines are process-wide singletons with process-wide state - the
#   platform, this thread's isolate, the suite's allocation counters. That state
#   is what the variables are; making them const would make them wrong.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-macro-usage
#   Answers "consider a constexpr function" to macros that could not be one: a
#   token paste, a stringify, and the suite's `UNIBIND_TEST_CASE`, whose job is
#   to *declare* a test case under a name built from its arguments.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-no-malloc
#   `tests/support/allocations.cpp` replaces the global allocation operators.
#   `malloc` and `free` are the only things it *can* call: a container or a
#   smart pointer there is unbounded recursion.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-owning-memory
#   Off because its answer is `gsl::owner<>` and this library depends on nothing
#   but the standard library and one engine - but every site was read first,
#   because ownership is where this tree's defects have been. Three kinds:
#
#     * The opaque-handle boundary, which is all but a few of them. `ContextRec`,
#       `ScriptRec`, `GlobalNode` and `NativeBox` are declared as raw pointers in
#       `detail/backend.h` *so that no engine type appears in a public header*:
#       one entry point makes each with `new` and the matching `Release`/`delete`
#       ends it. A `unique_ptr` cannot cross that boundary without putting the
#       deleter - and so the backend - in the header the boundary exists to keep
#       clean. Deliberate, documented, and covered by docs/lifetimes.md.
#     * Placement-new the check misreads as a heap allocation: `OpenFrame`
#       constructs a frame in storage the caller already owns and returns a
#       reference. Nothing is allocated and nothing is freed.
#     * `tests/support/allocations.cpp`, which *is* the allocator.
#
#   There was one genuine owning raw pointer - the V8 frame's lazily-allocated
#   `overflow` and `deferred` vectors, `new`ed in `Push`/`Escape` and `delete`d
#   by hand in `~Frame`. Those are `std::unique_ptr` now: same size, so the
#   frame's storage budget is unchanged, and two hand-written deletes gone.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-array-to-pointer-decay,
# cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-constant-array-index,
# cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-pointer-arithmetic
#   The same GSL answer to the same question, and again every site was read
#   rather than waved through. They are: walking `argv`, which the language
#   hands over as `char**`; the offset in a `memcpy` that writes a header and
#   then a payload; a UTF-8 validator indexing the bytes of a `string_view`; and
#   one lookup into a five-element `constexpr std::array` by a length that the
#   branch above it has already pinned to 2, 3 or 4. None of them is an
#   ownership or a lifetime question, and a `span` would move the same index
#   rather than remove it.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-const-cast, cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-reinterpret-cast
#   This is the boundary those rules exist to keep out of ordinary code, and
#   having one place for it is the point of the library. A handle is an opaque
#   pointer that has to become a `JS::Value*` or a `v8::Local<v8::Value>`, and
#   SpiderMonkey's C API takes non-const pointers to things it does not write.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-member-init
#   Three findings, all the same one: a constructor that deliberately leaves a
#   raw storage buffer alone because the next thing to touch it is a
#   placement-new. Zeroing it first would cost the memset and prove nothing.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-vararg
#   The variadic calls are the engines' own C entry points and the C runtime's
#   `printf`. Neither is ours to re-declare.
#
# modernize-use-designated-initializers
#   Fires on the engines' C aggregates - `JSClass`, `JSClassOps`, the proxy
#   handler - whose members are positional today and named by the engine, not by
#   us. Designating them means transcribing the engine's field names into this
#   tree and re-checking them on every engine bump, in exchange for nothing the
#   trailing comment beside each `nullptr` does not already say.
#
# modernize-use-nodiscard
#   All 24 findings are one fluent builder - `Class<T>`'s `Construct`, `Method`,
#   `Accessor`, `SetHandler` - whose methods return `*this` so they can be
#   chained. The standalone call `cls.Method("bump", &Bump);` is the ordinary
#   one, and `[[nodiscard]]` would make it a warning.
#
# modernize-use-std-print
#   Eight `printf`s in a benchmark's progress output. `<print>` is a large
#   include to add to a binary that already links a gigabyte of engine, for
#   lines going to stderr.
#
# modernize-use-trailing-return-type
#   493 findings, every one of them "write this declaration the other way
#   round". The tree writes leading return types throughout and its
#   `.clang-format` is settled; this is a whole-tree restyle nobody asked for.
#
# readability-function-cognitive-complexity
#   What it finds is a backend's dispatch over the engine's type space: a flat
#   switch with one arm per kind, which is long because the space is, and which
#   splitting would only scatter.
#
# readability-identifier-length
#   `cx` is what every line of SpiderMonkey's own documentation calls a
#   `JSContext*`, and a loop over slots indexes with `i`. Renaming to satisfy a
#   minimum would make the backend read less like the engine it wraps.
#
# readability-use-anyofallof
#   An opinion about loops, not a defect: it rewrites a plain early-return loop
#   into `std::ranges::any_of` with a lambda, which is longer here and no
#   clearer.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

CheckOptions:
  # A deliberately ignored result is spelled `(void)`; that is what the cast is
  # for, and the check should read it as the statement it is.
  bugprone-unused-return-value.AllowCastToVoid: true

  # An allocation failure is a state this suite *observes* rather than an error
  # it handles: it arms a failing allocator, lets the throw pass through, and
  # then asks what the ownership rules say. The catch is empty because there is
  # nothing to do in it, which is the finding. Any other exception type still
  # reports.
  bugprone-empty-catch.AllowEmptyCatchForExceptions: 'std::bad_alloc'

  # Keep the half of this check that finds a redundant boolean literal, drop the
  # half that rewrites `!(a && b)` into `!a || !b`. The predicates it fires on
  # here say "not a negative zero", and they read worse turned inside out.
  readability-simplify-boolean-expr.SimplifyDeMorgan: false

  # The analyzer cannot see across a translation unit, so every `Slot` that
  # comes back from a backend entry point - declared in a header, defined in the
  # backend - is an opaque struct it assumes is uninitialized, and passing one
  # to the next entry point is a hundred reports of the same non-fact. `Slot`
  # has a default member initializer on every field. The rest of CallAndMessage
  # - a call through a null function pointer, a method on a null object - still
  # reports.
  clang-analyzer-core.CallAndMessage:ArgInitializedness: false

# Our own headers, and only ours: an engine's headers and vcpkg's are not this
# tree's to fix, and the V8 and SpiderMonkey trees are large enough that leaving
# them in buries everything else.
HeaderFilterRegex: '.*[/\\]unibind[/\\](include|src|tests|tools)[/\\].*'
ExcludeHeaderFilterRegex: '.*[/\\](dependencies|vcpkg_installed|build|out)[/\\].*'

FormatStyle: file
